ents  through the the chocolate bar because in  any given year West Africa produces more  than half of the world's raw cocoa and  like one chocolate bar and a billion and  then take the Snickers bar into it the  peanuts the sugar it's all coming from  Africa but the bar itself is produced so  we talked about French roast coffee we  talked about Swiss chocolate or Dutch  processed cocoa  you know but the products themselves are  all Africa based so Africa cities live  with that sort of colonial hangover but  you know what so do many American cities  if we think about it in a in a different  sort of way and it's especially poignant  right now to think of Hartford in this  way because Hartford is very staunchly  and strongly a Puerto Rican City and  Puerto Rico just was signed over to an  oversight board that most of my Puerto  Rican friends view as neo-colonial that  is deciding on Puerto Rico's future and  the connections of Puerto Rico and  Hartford are very strong  one of the few places outside of the  continental United States that you can  fly to non-stop and direct and send what  from the Hartford Airport and then you  have all the other Caribbean people from  from from Jamaica from Trinidad from  from Dominican Republic who make up a  significant population within Hartford  and then I just throw in since we're  still in Connecticut many people from  Connecticut will know this but other  people who may not again depends on the  revenues and they've been following but  a sizable percentage of the state budget  of Connecticut comes from the revenues  paid into the state budget by the  casinos of the Pequot and Mohegan  reservations in eastern Connecticut so  even the indigenous people who are  virtual  nonexistent in the demography of  Connecticut still play a major role in  the the funding of the city so there are  ways in which this notion of colonialism  and post colonialism that is so dominant  in the understanding of Africa cities is  very relevant to my mind to to cities in  the u.s. at cities like like Harford  poverty and inequality there's a measure  that we use in development studies in  economics it's called a Gini coefficient  I'm not going to get into the details of  it some of you will probably have seen  this before it's a measure of income  inequality it can be used to measure  other forms of inequality as well but  income inequality on and it's normalized  to a number between 0 and 1 and so most  of the countries in Western Europe when  you look at the Gini coefficient of  income inequality of the cities of  Western Europe the number is somewhere  around point 3 to 0.4 there are no  cities below point 3 in the world that I  can think of of any significance you  always can have inequality so 0.3 is  considered to be a very low rate of  inequality many cities in Africa have a  rate between point three and point four  in their Gini coefficient of income  inequality the major exceptions are in  southern Africa South Africa especially  Gini coefficients of income inequality  in South Africa lead the world more on  the scale of 0.61 in Cape Town but  that's that's actually down from where  it had been under apartheid these  numbers were you know 0.75 0.78 so just  unbelievable levels of income inequality  but most African cities are more in that  0.4 range most US cities are above 0.4  and we happen to have a number  of of the highest cities of inequality  in the United States right around us and  I'll get to that in a minute but a  Hartford which has a lower Gini  coefficient than some other US cities  certainly Bridgeport fare Fairfield  which is the most unequal urban area in  the United States on on most measures  but Hartford is also a place with a  metro area that's very massively wealthy  and there have been measures that have  come out that show it is one of the  world's wealthiest metropolitan areas so  what might not show up in the Gini  coefficient for the city itself shows up  when you look more broadly at the  spatial inequalities of the city which  are very much to me like you would see  in in Africa so Nairobi has a Gini  coefficient of 0.57 the most recent one  for bridge boys was 0.5 for 3 but it had  been at point seven five point five  seven New York City which is one of the  top five unequal cities the United  States and income inequality that's  equal to Accra and when we look at South  Africa which is off the charts but we  see cities in South Africa having the  Gini coefficient going down they have  lots of problems in cities in South  Africa but as Martin was suggesting with  that rising middle class black  population that's a key feature of  decreasing the Gini coefficient so if  we're concerned as it seems to be in the  United States with inequality as a  rising issue in our cities why not look  at a place like South Africa not to say  cookie cutter let's take what they did  and do it in Connecticut but let's say  what lessons are there for cities in the  United States of a society like South  Africa and Brazil is another example  where you have declining Gini  coefficients are there any things that  have been done  there that seemed like not such a bad  thing this just gives you a little map  about you see some cities around it's  New Haven's not so so hot a little bit  lower than Hartford and then Fairfield  at the top of the charts and there are  other measures and other elements of  this as I said you have you know more  than 40 percent of the population living  below the poverty level in Hartford you  have by some measures half it's gone  down to about 40 percent as I understand  it because of Obamacare but somewhere  above forty percent of all children  lacking health insurance and this is  Hartford Connecticut which is the  insurance city it's one of the most if  our first shows up on those charts of  global and world cities it's for the  insurance industry obviously so when we  look at cities in Africa we say they're  unequal and there's there's severe  issues of poverty what what can we take  from those cities and apply in in Africa  I mean in Hartford just to give us a  space because I'm a geographer of the  spatial aspect of this this is a rough  outline of the boundaries of the City of  Hartford and it's very fundamentally  divided between a black north and a  Latino south with a scattering of white  population a very very tiny Asian  population and a white West End and the  suburbs overwhelmingly white 78% of the  metropolitan statistical area of  Hartford is right and about 14% of the  city itself is white we see a spillover  effect as a growth of black middle-class  the United States leads to cities like  Bloomfield and Windsor to the north of  Hartford becoming significantly that the  school systems in both of those cities  now have a black majority so we have  right you know in our own state here a  very significant case of segregation  which is something that as a scholar of  colonial influence  was a very clear and deliberate tactic  of British colonialism in Africa's  cities was it was it so clear and  deliberate in the United States it seems  something to contemplate and then we get  on to the corrupt and underfunded local  government let me first talk about  Africa we have long cases of people just  falling to get funding in cities to the  city of Dar Salaam when I interviewed  the city commissioner about 12 years ago  showed me a chart where they had never  ever in the at that point seven years of  existence of the City Commission they  had never received more than 76 percent  of the money budgeted to them by the  United Republic of Tanzania government  and that budget was never enough in the  my solid waste management book one of  the things that again if this was the  director of the dump pointed out to me  it's a city where they received all  sorts of accolades around the world for  their solid waste management program  that's why I studied it in in dar Salam  it went from three percent of municipal  solid waste residential solid waste  being collected and sent to a landfill  to 40 percent and in my interview with  the dump manager he said well let's  let's let's be real here  we're not doing all that well think  about it what happens to the other 60%  and you see it everywhere it's burning  around you it's it's in every ditch and  and he said and it just says 40 percent  or 43 percent goes to a landfill let's  go to that landfill I'll show you what  that landfill is like and it's not a  protected landfill nobody working there  has any protective equipment you have  just horrific things leaching into the  water supply right from that landfill  which is on fire  so these have real consequences in  people's health everyday and I like to  talk about garbage so as most people  don't but to me it's sort of a way into  if the city works its garbage is being  collected and and people are not dying  of diseases that are easily preventable  if you collect the garbage so in those  contexts most of the time African cities  have been forced into privatization  schemes of one sort or another not as  Extreme as what Martin was talking about  earlier but in the sphere of solid waste  the the World Bank and the donor nations  all came and said what you got to do is  privatize this you got to have companies  that collect solid waste and it sounds  great when you come from a capitalist  country and you've been raised in a  society where uh of course there's a  garbage man he comes with a garbage  truck from a company that's been  contracted to a city and they collect  the garbage and they take it away to a  nice landfill most African cities have a  sizable percentage of the population  living in informal settlements which  don't have roads which have paths or  alleys that are not wide enough for  those trucks so the first thing that all  those programs had to do was to start  community-based enterprises that would  have wheelbarrows to collect the waste  and then